 "answer": "Yes, it's bad. Air quality in KL is at the \"unhealthy\" level today.\n\n**KL readings (Thursday, Oct 16, as of 5:30pm)**\n- **Batu Muda:** API 163, unhealthy\n- **Cheras:** API 163, unhealthy\n- **Putrajaya:** API 170, unhealthy\n\n**Around the Klang Valley**\nSelangor is about as bad or a little worse: Johan Setia is at 184, Klang 176, Petaling Jaya 173 and Shah Alam 168. The worst readings in the country are in Johor, where Larkin and Pasir Gudang reached 202–207. That's in the \"very unhealthy\" range. In total, 29 areas across Malaysia are at unhealthy levels or worse.\n\n**What the API numbers mean:** 0–50 is good, 51–100 moderate, 101–200 unhealthy, 201–300 very unhealthy and above 300 hazardous.\n\n**Background:** The haze is smoke from forest and peatland fires in Indonesia. It has been going on for over a week. On Oct 9, the government closed schools in KL, Putrajaya, Selangor and several other states, which affected about 2 million students.\n\n**What to do**\n- Spend less time outdoors, especially if you plan to exercise.\n- If you have to go out, wear an N95 mask.\n- Keep windows closed, and use an air purifier if you have one.\n- Be especially careful with children, older people and anyone with asthma, heart or lung conditions.\n\nReadings change every hour. For the latest numbers at your nearest station, check the Department of Environment's APIMS site (apims.doe.gov.my) or the MyIPU app.",
